---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.5 — Loop Analysis, Fencepost Traps &amp; The LoopTracer Build</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topics 2.1, 2.9 · 90-Minute Design-First Build.</strong> Nearly a quarter of AP CS A
    Multiple Choice questions ask you to predict how many times a loop executes, spot an off-by-one
    ("fencepost") error, or trace a loop whose state rises and falls inside the same iteration.
    Today you will design and build <code>LoopTracer.java</code>—a telemetry and simulation module
    built to defeat the exam's toughest loop-analysis traps on your very first run.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Design-First Workflow (One-Shot Challenge):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/LoopTracer.java" download>LoopTracer.java</a> — starter file with
            Javadocs, method stubs, and a built-in <strong>Dungeon Telemetry &amp; Mana
            Simulator</strong> in <code>main()</code>.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:0.5rem; margin-bottom:0.25rem;">
        <strong>How to work today:</strong>
    </p>
    <ol style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
        <li><strong>Design before coding:</strong> Trace small values on paper and answer the Guided
            Design questions on guard order, fencepost structure, intra-turn step ordering, and
            infinite-loop detection.</li>
        <li><strong>Draft &amp; simulate in the sandbox:</strong> Draft your method code in the box,
            click <strong>Copy</strong> to paste it into <code>LoopTracer.java</code>, and check
            your simulation outputs with:</li>
    </ol>
    <pre><code>javac LoopTracer.java
java LoopTracer</code></pre>
    <p style="margin-top:0.4rem; margin-bottom:0;">
        <strong>Step 3 (One-Shot Pass):</strong> Once your sandbox telemetry matches your paper
        traces, unlock <code>LoopTracerCheck.java</code> below and aim to pass all
        <strong>17 checks</strong> on your very first run.
    </p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 1 · Loop Guards &amp; Termination Analysis</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>countWhileIterations(int start, int limit, int step)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            Before executing an arbitrary spell-charge loop like:
        </p>
        <pre><code>int val = start;
while (val &lt; limit) {
    val += step;
}</code></pre>
        <p>
            the engine's Telemetry Guard predicts <em>how many times the body will execute</em>—and
            refuses to hang if a bad <code>step</code> would cause an infinite loop.
        </p>
        <h4>Parameters &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>int start</code> — initial value of <code>val</code> before the loop begins.</li>
            <li><code>int limit</code> — exclusive upper bound (<code>val &lt; limit</code>).</li>
            <li><code>int step</code> — amount added to <code>val</code> each iteration (may be
                positive, <code>0</code>, or negative!).</li>
            <li><strong>Returns (<code>int</code>):</strong> exact number of loop iterations,
                <code>0</code> if the loop never enters, or <code>-1</code> if it would run forever.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Walkthrough Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>(0, 10, 3)</code> &rarr; <code>4</code> (<code>val</code> visits
                <code>0, 3, 6, 9</code>; stops at <code>12</code>).</li>
            <li><code>(2, 10, 4)</code> &rarr; <code>2</code> (<code>val</code> visits
                <code>2, 6</code>; stops when <code>val == 10</code> because
                <code>10 &lt; 10</code> is <code>false</code>).</li>
            <li><code>(12, 10, 0)</code> &rarr; <code>0</code> (condition <code>12 &lt; 10</code>
                is immediately <code>false</code>, so <code>step == 0</code> never executes!).</li>
            <li><code>(0, 10, 0)</code> or <code>(0, 10, -2)</code> &rarr; <code>-1</code>
                (starts inside the loop and never progresses toward <code>limit</code>).</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>countWhileIterations</code></h3>
        <p>Reason through the guard priority and loop structure before coding:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Guard Priority (Edge Case):</strong> Suppose you check
                <code>if (step &lt;= 0) return -1;</code> <em>before</em> checking
                <code>if (start &gt;= limit) return 0;</code>. Which walkthrough example breaks that
                order, and what exact order of <code>if</code> guards prevents both false
                <code>-1</code>s and infinite loops?</li>
            <li><strong>Local Variables &amp; Simulation Loop:</strong> Once past the guards, what
                local variables do you declare, and what <code>while</code> loop counts the exact
                number of executions without an off-by-one error on <code>(2, 10, 4)</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>LoopTracer.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>countWhileIterations</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>LoopTracer.java</code>, and check the sandbox output for
                <code>(0, 10, 3)</code>, <code>(2, 10, 4)</code>, <code>(12, 10, 0)</code>, and
                <code>(0, 10, -2)</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 2 · The Fencepost Pattern</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>buildRunedFence(String rune, int pillars)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            To mark a warded arena border, the builder strings together <code>pillars</code> rune
            posts (<code>"[" + rune + "]"</code>) linked by <code>"---"</code> energy spans. Just
            like a real fence with 3 posts has only 2 rails between them, joining <em>N</em> items
            with <em>N - 1</em> separators is the classic <strong>Fencepost Problem</strong>.
        </p>
        <h4>Parameters &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>String rune</code> (precondition: <code>rune != null</code>) — label inside
                each pillar.</li>
            <li><code>int pillars</code> — number of pillars to place.</li>
            <li><strong>Returns (<code>String</code>):</strong> <code>pillars</code> posts connected
                by <code>pillars - 1</code> dividers (no leading or trailing <code>"---"</code>), or
                <code>""</code> if <code>pillars &lt;= 0</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Walkthrough Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>("*", 3)</code> &rarr; <code>"[*]---[*]---[*]"</code> (3 posts, 2 dividers).</li>
            <li><code>("SOL", 1)</code> &rarr; <code>"[SOL]"</code> (1 post, 0 dividers).</li>
            <li><code>("*", 0)</code> or <code>("*", -2)</code> &rarr; <code>""</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>buildRunedFence</code></h3>
        <p>Design a clean fencepost loop with zero trailing separators:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>The Naive-Loop Trap:</strong> Why does a simple loop running
                <code>pillars</code> times and appending <code>"[" + rune + "]---"</code> fail on
                <code>("*", 3)</code>? How does placing the <em>first</em> post before the loop (and
                looping <code>pillars - 1</code> times to append <code>"---[" + rune + "]"</code>)
                solve the fencepost problem?</li>
            <li><strong>Edge-Case Guard:</strong> If you place the first post before the loop, why
                must you add an <code>if (pillars &lt;= 0)</code> guard at the very top of
                <code>buildRunedFence</code> before initializing <code>result</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>LoopTracer.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>buildRunedFence</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>LoopTracer.java</code>, and check the sandbox banners for
                <code>("*", 3)</code>, <code>("SOL", 1)</code>, and <code>("*", 0)</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 3 · Periodic Cycles &amp; Peak-Before-Drain</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>firstTurnReachingMana(int startMana, int gainPerTurn, int drainEveryThirdTurn, int targetMana)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            A cursed wizard regenerates mana every turn, but every 3rd turn a curse siphons away a
            burst of energy. Your turn simulator determines the exact turn number when the wizard
            finally hits <code>targetMana</code> to cast their escape spell—or detects that the
            curse permanently traps them below the goal.
        </p>
        <h4>Turn Simulation Rules &amp; Parameters</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li>If <code>startMana &gt;= targetMana</code> before Turn 1, return <code>0</code>.</li>
            <li>On each turn <code>turn = 1, 2, 3, ...</code>:
                <ol>
                    <li>Add <code>gainPerTurn</code> to <code>mana</code>.</li>
                    <li>If <code>turn % 3 == 0</code>, subtract <code>drainEveryThirdTurn</code>.</li>
                    <li>At the end of the turn, if <code>mana &gt;= targetMana</code>, return
                        <code>turn</code>.</li>
                </ol>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Returns (<code>int</code>):</strong> first winning turn number,
                <code>0</code> if already at target, or <code>-1</code> if <code>targetMana</code>
                can never be reached.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Walkthrough Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>(0, 5, 4, 15)</code> &rarr; <code>4</code> (T1: <code>5</code>, T2:
                <code>10</code>, T3: <code>10 + 5 - 4 = 11</code>, T4: <code>11 + 5 = 16 &gt;= 15</code>).</li>
            <li><code>(6, 2, 10, 10)</code> &rarr; <code>2</code> (3-turn net is negative
                <code>6 - 10 = -4</code>, yet Turn 1 reaches <code>8</code> and Turn 2 reaches
                <code>10</code> <em>before</em> Turn 3's drain hits!).</li>
            <li><code>(0, 2, 6, 10)</code> &rarr; <code>-1</code> (every 3 turns nets
                <code>0</code>, so mana cycles <code>2, 4, 0, 2, 4, 0...</code> forever).</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>firstTurnReachingMana</code></h3>
        <p>Analyze loop choice, order inside a turn, and the early-peak trap:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Loop Choice &amp; Order of Operations:</strong> Why must this use a
                <code>while</code> loop rather than a fixed <code>for</code> loop? Inside one turn
                (using Turn 3 of <code>(0, 5, 4, 15)</code> as proof), why would checking
                <code>mana &gt;= targetMana</code> <em>before</em> subtracting
                <code>drainEveryThirdTurn</code> return the wrong turn number?</li>
            <li><strong>Stall Detection Edge Case:</strong> Why does checking
                <code>if (3 * gainPerTurn - drainEveryThirdTurn &lt;= 0) return -1;</code> at the
                top of the method fail on <code>(6, 2, 10, 10)</code>? Why is it safe to simulate at
                least through Turn 3 and only return <code>-1</code> if at the end of Turn 3 the
                mage hasn't won and <code>mana &lt;= startMana</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>LoopTracer.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>firstTurnReachingMana</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>LoopTracer.java</code>, and verify all four walkthrough scenarios in
                <code>java LoopTracer</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 4 · Non-Unit Step Nested Loop Tracing</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 4: <code>countTriangularSteps(int n)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            AP Multiple Choice questions love pairing a dependent inner start (<code>j = i</code>)
            with a non-unit step (<code>j += 2</code>). Writing an instrumented counter lets you
            verify your hand-trace tables against actual Java execution.
        </p>
        <pre><code>int steps = 0;
for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; i++) {
    for (int j = i; j &lt;= n; j += 2) {
        steps++;
    }
}</code></pre>
        <h4>Parameters &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>int n</code> — upper bound of both loops (if <code>n &lt;= 0</code>, return
                <code>0</code>).</li>
            <li><strong>Returns (<code>int</code>):</strong> total executions of
                <code>steps++</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>countTriangularSteps</code></h3>
        <p>Hand-trace the loop pairs before implementing:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Hand Trace:</strong> List the exact <code>j</code> values visited for each
                <code>i</code> when <code>n = 4</code> and when <code>n = 5</code>, and state the
                total <code>steps</code> for both (plus how <code>i &lt;= n</code> handles
                <code>n &lt;= 0</code> without a separate guard)?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>LoopTracer.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your <code>countTriangularSteps</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>LoopTracer.java</code>, and check <code>java LoopTracer</code> against your
                paper trace before unlocking <code>LoopTracerCheck.java</code> below?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>All four methods produce expected traces in <code>java LoopTracer</code></li>
            <li>You unlock <code>LoopTracerCheck.java</code> below and aim for <strong><code>17 passed, 0 failed</code></strong> on your first run</li>
            <li>You have downloaded your <code>.json</code> answers file for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/loop-analysis.html">Tracing Loops</a> — trace tables, off-by-one, iteration counts</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="4.5" title="4.5 Loop Analysis, Fencepost Traps &amp; LoopTracer Build — AP CS A" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
